  1	//! Claims files: a plain-text way to write a DAG of claims.
  2	//!
  3	//! One row per piece of evidence, six tab-separated columns:
  4	//!
  5	//!   label  rests_on  witness  statement  path  quote
  6	//!
  7	//! - `label` names the claim within this file (e.g. `C3`). Rows sharing a
  8	//!   label are one claim; their statement and witness must match.
  9	//! - `rests_on` is `-` or a comma-separated list of labels in this file:
 10	//!   the claims this one is built from.
 11	//! - `path` is a repository file, ingested as an artifact; `quote` must occur
 12	//!   in it exactly once (`\t`, `\n`, `\\` are unescaped first). Both are `-`
 13	//!   for a row that adds no evidence. The path must be relative, made of
 14	//!   plain components only (no `..`, `.`, empty or absolute parts), contain
 15	//!   no symlink, and resolve inside the repository: otherwise a claims file
 16	//!   could pull `/etc/hosts` or `~/.ssh/id_ed25519` into the graph, and the
 17	//!   quote check would become an oracle for guessing their contents.
 18	//!
 19	//! Lines that are blank or start with `#` are ignored.
 20	//!
 21	//! Problems are collected per row, not raised at the first one: a proposal
 22	//! from an LLM is expected to contain errors, and the point is to see all of
 23	//! them. A claim whose evidence all failed, or that rests on a failed claim,
 24	//! fails too.
 25	
 26	use crate::model::{Claim, Evidence, Link, media_type_for};
 27	use crate::store::Store;
 28	use crate::{Error, Result};
 29	use std::collections::{BTreeMap, BTreeSet};
 30	use std::fs;
 31	use std::path::{Component, Path, PathBuf};
